1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,316
Every picture in the last
act leaned on the risk model.

2
00:00:03,356 --> 00:00:05,955
So here is a comforting fact about it.

3
00:00:05,995 --> 00:00:08,818
For every one of our two hundred providers,

4
00:00:08,858 --> 00:00:12,937
observed equals expected,
exactly. Every meter is level.

5
00:00:13,067 --> 00:00:18,446
Here is a second crowd, fitted the same
way. Its two hundred meters are level too.

6
00:00:18,486 --> 00:00:23,145
One of these two models is badly wrong.
From here, you cannot tell which.

7
00:00:23,267 --> 00:00:26,667
That is because the fit
itself levels these meters.

8
00:00:26,707 --> 00:00:30,630
Episode 6 showed how: balancing
is what fitting means.

9
00:00:30,670 --> 00:00:33,702
A check that every model
passes is no check at all.

10
00:00:33,742 --> 00:00:36,502
We need to cut the patients another way.

11
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:40,321
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

12
00:00:40,361 --> 00:00:42,653
Today: trusting the risk model.

13
00:00:44,733 --> 00:00:47,607
Forget the providers for a moment.

14
00:00:47,647 --> 00:00:55,210
Line up all 28,848 patients by the risk the
model gives them, from lowest to highest.

15
00:00:55,250 --> 00:01:00,144
Cut the line into ten equal groups,
and give each group a meter of its own.

16
00:01:00,267 --> 00:01:04,933
For the first crowd, the
ten meters barely tilt.

17
00:01:04,973 --> 00:01:09,763
Observed over expected runs from 0.95 to 1.05.

18
00:01:09,803 --> 00:01:14,014
Every one of those is within what
luck allows. This model passes.

19
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:23,992
Now the second crowd. In its lowest-risk
tenth, the model expected 136 readmissions.

20
00:01:24,032 --> 00:01:28,293
There were 339: two and a half times as many.

21
00:01:28,333 --> 00:01:33,185
In the middle, the meters tilt the other
way: more than a fifth fewer than expected.

22
00:01:33,333 --> 00:01:35,803
Here is what went wrong. In this crowd,

23
00:01:35,843 --> 00:01:39,985
risk does not rise in a straight
line with the first risk factor.

24
00:01:40,025 --> 00:01:45,709
It bends: patients at either extreme are
at higher risk than a straight line allows.

25
00:01:45,749 --> 00:01:49,384
The model was given a straight
line, and did the best it could.

26
00:01:49,533 --> 00:01:56,923
Give it the bend, as a squared term, and
the ten meters settle: from 0.92 to 1.08.

27
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:01,630
Did the bend matter for the providers? It did.

28
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:05,671
Between the two fits, 16 of
the 200 change their flag.

29
00:02:05,711 --> 00:02:11,326
And the fifth of providers with the most
unusual case mix had ratios 16% too high.

30
00:02:11,366 --> 00:02:14,957
They were being blamed
for the model's mistake.

31
00:02:16,533 --> 00:02:20,760
There is a famous one-number
summary of a risk model: the AUC.

32
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:24,614
Take one patient who was
readmitted and one who was not.

33
00:02:24,654 --> 00:02:29,683
The AUC is the chance that the model
gave the first the higher risk.

34
00:02:29,833 --> 00:02:36,321
For the wrong model, it is 0.74.
For the corrected one, 0.76.

35
00:02:36,361 --> 00:02:40,139
A tenth of the patients were off
by a factor of two and a half,

36
00:02:40,179 --> 00:02:45,436
and the AUC barely noticed. It measures
ranking, not whether the risks are right.

37
00:02:45,567 --> 00:02:52,730
One more caution. In the first crowd, the
AUC is 0.72 from the risk factors alone,

38
00:02:52,770 --> 00:02:57,794
and 0.74 once the model knows
each patient's provider.

39
00:02:57,834 --> 00:03:02,770
That gain says providers differ. It does
not say the risk rule got any better.

40
00:03:04,367 --> 00:03:10,444
A third cut: inside one provider. Our
largest has 484 patients, and for them,

41
00:03:10,484 --> 00:03:16,792
in this experiment, risk climbs twice
as steeply as the shared rule allows.

42
00:03:16,933 --> 00:03:24,125
Its total balances, as it must:
143 observed, 143 expected.

43
00:03:24,165 --> 00:03:30,268
But split its patients into a
lower-risk half and a higher-risk half.

44
00:03:30,308 --> 00:03:33,774
18 against 41. And 125 against 102.

45
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:36,328
One gamma cannot describe this provider.

46
00:03:36,368 --> 00:03:39,721
It is an average over the
patients it happens to treat,

47
00:03:39,761 --> 00:03:42,826
and a direct rate built on it would mislead.

48
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:48,330
Software can catch some trouble
before fitting: missing values,

49
00:03:48,370 --> 00:03:53,006
a risk factor that never varies,
two that say nearly the same thing.

50
00:03:53,133 --> 00:03:56,799
But every check today compared
the model with data we have.

51
00:03:56,839 --> 00:04:00,133
None can see a risk factor
that nobody recorded.

52
00:04:00,173 --> 00:04:04,774
And more is not always better. Adjust
for something a provider causes,

53
00:04:04,814 --> 00:04:09,142
such as a complication, and you
adjust its performance away.

54
00:04:10,733 --> 00:04:12,229
Let's gather it up.

55
00:04:12,269 --> 00:04:16,368
Provider meters are level by
construction. They prove nothing.

56
00:04:16,408 --> 00:04:21,680
So take the same meter to new groups: tenths
of risk, and halves within a provider.

57
00:04:21,720 --> 00:04:24,715
And the AUC measures ranking, not fairness.

58
00:04:24,833 --> 00:04:30,222
Throughout this series, each provider has had
a number of its own, estimated on its own.

59
00:04:30,262 --> 00:04:35,908
Many analysts would do it differently, and
pull the small providers toward the average.

60
00:04:35,948 --> 00:04:38,260
Next time: fixed or random?

61
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:42,505
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
